'''Limón''' ({{Literal translation|{{W|lemon}}}}; {{Langx|gug|Ka'a-Yva-Pirãi|4=forest of sour fruit}}) is a [[Boroughs of Castejón|borough]] of the [[Castejón Metropolis]], within the [[Concordia|Concordia conurbation]]. The borough is the ninth-most populous; with a recorded population of 1,273,351 residents in [[2025 Baliscan census|2025]]. Limón was an independent commune until October 1902, when it [[consolidation of Castejón|was consolidated]] with other [[Alpujarra|Alpujarran communes]] to form the modern Castejón Metropolis. Prior to consolidation, the Metropolis lay entirely on the western shore of [[Salto Bay]], with six constituent boroughs. The borough continues to maintain a [[Culture of Limón|distinct culture]]: many barrios are {{W|ethnic enclave}}s, such as [[Poko Yanbu]] and [[Hachijoko]].

The name "Limón" originated with the lemon farms were established of the area, as was the nearby [[Misión de Vall de las Limónes]]. The indigenous [[Kuarupíba]] people had been displaced by Mesogean settlers by 1632. In the 19th and 20th centuries, Limón received many immigrant and migrant groups as it was transformed into an urban community, first from various Mesogean countries (particularly from the Empire of [[Burawa]] [specifically from [[Dakare]] and [[Ayorin]]], [[Tamazgha]], [[Andalus]]) and later from the [[Fosia]] continent (particularly [[Daras]], [[Kimcang]], [[Yanbaru]], and [[Okaiken]]). This cultural mix has made Limón a wellspring of {{W|Latin music|Abyalan music}}, {{W|Afrobeats|Kaftobeats}} and [[Sanese Drill]].

